    "adaptations": "Foundation: shorter distance, slower pace, larger target, and softer equipment.\nIntermediate: standard distance and one decision cue.\nAdvanced: smaller target, weaker side, or later cue without increasing contact.\nLarge group: duplicate simple zones and use performer–observer–recorder trios.\nSmall group: fewer zones and more feedback cycles.\nLimited space: stationary practice and short one-way routes.\nPartial equipment: one item per pair; observer rehearses movement without equipment.\nDifferent needs: adapt position, range, distance, pace, ball size, role, and communication; allow seated or fixed-support participation.",
    "assessment": "Teacher checks: close ball control while dribbling, scanning before choosing a direction, the sole stop on signal, changing direction without entering another student's space, and taking both the performer and observer roles with factual feedback.\nSuccess: at least four of the five measures are shown consistently.\nPartial success: safe execution but a prompt is needed for scanning or the observer role.\nNot yet successful: ball control or safe spacing remains unsafe after adaptation; return to isolated foundation practice.",
    "reflection": "Teacher questions: Who prepared early? Did the organisation create enough practice? Which adaptation improved control?\nStudent questions: What cue helped? What changed after feedback? What will you repeat next time?\nImprovement: adjust one distance, target, group ratio, or cue before FB-02 — pass, receive, and support movement.",
    "continuity": "If outcomes are met: proceed to FB-02 — pass, receive, and support movement.\nIf not: repeat the foundation action with no decision pressure, one success cue, and a larger target.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: Football, school PE, foundation, safety, learning pathway.\nSport: Football. Age: 9–11. Equipment: basic.\nSkills: preparation, control, accuracy, decision, and feedback.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: foundation.\nKeywords: close touches, inside-foot touch, outside-foot touch, sole stop, scanning, free space, change of direction, foundation football.",
    "systemData": "Physical load: moderate\nCognitive load: moderate\nOrganisational load: low–moderate\nStudent independence: moderate",
    "teacherSummary": "3 minutes for goal and safety; 7 for progressive warm-up.\n10 minutes to establish the central technique.\n12 minutes to apply it with one decision.\n8 minutes of inclusive process-scored play.\n5 minutes for feedback, recovery, and collection.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Every later football skill assumes the ball can be moved without being watched; close control is what frees the eyes, and free eyes are what make passing, turning, and finishing possible.\nWhat develops? Fine foot–ball coordination on both sides of the foot, the sole stop as a reliable brake, visual scanning while moving, and early judgement about which space is safe to enter.\nWhy not skip? Students who begin with games instead of control kick the ball ahead and chase it; that pattern rewards the fastest runner rather than the best player, and it excludes everyone who is not quick.",
    "selfQualityCheck": "✅ Completely original\n✅ Safety reviewed\n✅ Observable objective included\n✅ Age appropriate\n✅ Realistic school equipment\n✅ Learning pathway included\n✅ Adaptations included\n✅ Assessment included\n✅ Reflection included\n✅ Ready to teach in the field\nStatus: original V2 draft; professional review, language editing, and field pilot are required before publication.",
